\begin_layout Section
The stabilizer formalism
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:The-stabilizer-formalism"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Minimal Working Example
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a two bit Shor code (i.e., two qubit system) and the standard 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{z}^{i}$
\end_inset

 Pauli operators, where 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 indicates which qubit the operator will act on.
 What are the outcomes of applying 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{z}^{1}\sigma_{z}^{2}$
\end_inset

 to the possible codes?
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\sigma_{z}^{1}\sigma_{z}^{2}\ket{00} & =(+1\times+1)\ket{00}=\ket{00}\\
\sigma_{z}^{1}\sigma_{z}^{2}\ket{11} & =(-1\times-1)\ket{11}=\ket{11}\\
\sigma_{z}^{1}\sigma_{z}^{2}\ket{10} & =(-1\times+1)\ket{10}=-\ket{10}\\
\sigma_{z}^{1}\sigma_{z}^{2}\ket{01} & =(+1\times-1)\ket{01}=-\ket{01}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Thus, we observe that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{z}^{1}\sigma_{z}^{2}$
\end_inset

 can pick up 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bit flips
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the code.
 The same approach (though using 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}^{i}$
\end_inset

 Pauli operators) can also be used to pick up 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

phase
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 differences between codes; for example, let
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\psi & :=\ket{000}+\ket{111}\\
\phi & :=\ket{000}-\ket{111}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\sigma_{x}^{1}\sigma_{x}^{2}\sigma_{x}^{3}\psi & =\psi\\
\sigma_{x}^{1}\sigma_{x}^{2}\sigma_{x}^{3}\phi & =-\phi
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the above two examples, the operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{z}^{1}\sigma_{z}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{x}^{1}\sigma_{x}^{2}\sigma_{x}^{3}$
\end_inset

 are said to 
\emph on
stabilize 
\emph default
the respective logical codes, i.e., the states corresponding to the encoding
 of logical 0 (
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{00}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{000}$
\end_inset

) or logical 1 (
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{11}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{111}$
\end_inset

) are eigenstates with eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

.
 Note, these ideas generalize to larger and larger Shor codes
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.quant-ph/9705052"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 but in non-obvious ways (in terms of exactly which Pauli operators should
 be applied).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Stabilizer subgroup
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Generalizing these examples, we're looking for members of a subgroup within
 the Pauli group 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{k}$
\end_inset

 (on 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 qubits) called the 
\emph on
stabilizer
\emph default
 group (
\begin_inset Formula $S_{k}$
\end_inset

).
 The defining property of 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{k}$
\end_inset

 is that for any 
\begin_inset Formula $g\in S_{k}\subset G_{k}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
g\ket{0}_{k} & =+1\ket{0}_{k}\\
g\ket{1}_{k} & =-1\ket{1}_{k}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where the subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 indicates 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{0}_{k}:=\ket{\underbrace{00\cdots00}_{k}}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
A key property of the stabilizer group is that it is abelian
\series default
, i.e., all of the members commute with each others.
 This enables us to simultaneously measure
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If operators 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 commute, then you can come up with a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

complete
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 set of basis states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{f_{n}g_{n}}$
\end_inset

 that are eigenstates of both 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

, and thus express any state of the quantum system as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=\sum_{n}\alpha_{n}\ket{f_{n}g_{n}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Measuring 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 you will get eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{m}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\alpha_{m}^{2}\right|$
\end_inset

 and the system will be in eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{f_{m}g_{m}}$
\end_inset

 and similarly for 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, it doesn't matter which order you measure 
\begin_inset Formula $F,G$
\end_inset

 in, you will get a consistent result.
 This is the significance of commuting operators.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 all of the 
\begin_inset Formula $g\in S_{k}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The code space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Okay, but this description kind of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

puts the cart before the horse
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 We described how to detect errors in codes without really describing the
 encoding/code-space - we mention Shor codes but those actually aren't the
 only ones we can correct using this scheme.
 In general, the code space is the 
\emph on
linear subspace
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{S_{k}}$
\end_inset

 of all possible 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-qubit states in the intersection of all the 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 eigenspaces of 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{k}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{S_{k}}:=\left\{ \ket{\psi}\big\lvert\,g\ket{\psi}=+1\ket{\psi}\,\forall g\in S_{k}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

That's a lot of fancy words but basically this means 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{S_{k}}$
\end_inset

 consists of all sums
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\phi}=\sum_{k}\alpha_{k}\ket{\psi}
\]

\end_inset

where each 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
stabilized by all
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall g\in S_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In general 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{k}$
\end_inset

 is completely described by its 
\emph on

\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 
\emph default
independent/unique 
\emph on
generators
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A generating set 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle S\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 of a group 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is a subset of the group set such that every element of the group can be
 expressed as a combination (under the group operation) of finitely many
 elements of the subset and their inverses.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle g_{1},\dots,g_{l}\right\rangle $
\end_inset


\emph default
.
 You can rigorously prove that the dimension
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
I.e., dimension of the vector space of legal 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 length qubit states.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{S_{k}}$
\end_inset

 is equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{k-l}$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left|T_{S_{k}}\right|=2^{k-l}
\]

\end_inset

but intuitively you can think of it like this: for a 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 dimensional code space, without any constraints (i.e., just by default),
 you have 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{k}$
\end_inset

 basis states (independent, legal, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 length qubit states).
 But then each stabilizer 
\begin_inset Formula $g\in S_{k}$
\end_inset

 constrains 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 to be in the 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

-eigenspace of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 and thereby cuts out the possibilities by half and hence after constraining
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 stabilizers
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
2^{k}\times\underbrace{\frac{1}{2}\frac{1}{2}\cdots\frac{1}{2}}_{l}=2^{k-l}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Vertex Operators and Plaquette Operators
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename fig1_intro_toric_codes.png
	lyxscale 50
	width 70text%

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2D Lattice with periodic boundary conditions with .
 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 marks a particular vertex of the lattice and 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 marks a particular 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

face
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of the lattice.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "2dlattice"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a 2D square lattice with periodic boundary conditions
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
i.e., a torus
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and single qubit, spin-
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 particles
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
e.g., proton, neutron, electron, neutrino
\end_layout

\end_inset

 on each edge (see figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "2dlattice"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 Take the standard 
\begin_inset Formula $X:=\sigma_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z:=\sigma_{z}$
\end_inset

 Pauli operators.
 Assume the standard convention, that 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0},\ket{1}$
\end_inset

are eigenstates of 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

, defined such that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
Z\ket{0} & =+1\ket{0}\\
Z\ket{1} & =-1\ket{1}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Note that in the 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 basis, the 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 operator acts as a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

spin-flip
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 operator:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
X\ket{0} & =\ket{1}\\
X\ket{1} & =\ket{0}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Then define 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

vertex
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

plaquette
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (face) operators 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
A_{v} & :=\prod_{j\in\text{star}\left(v\right)}Z_{j}\\
B_{p} & :=\prod_{j\in\text{body}\left(p\right)}X_{j}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{star}\left(v\right)$
\end_inset

 is the set of particles identified with edges adjacent on the vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{body}\left(p\right)$
\end_inset

 is the set of particles identified with the edges surrounding the plaquette
 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 Note the product here is a tensor product.
 Then define the following Hamiltonian
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H:=-\sum_{v}A_{v}-\sum_{p}B_{p}\label{eq:hamil}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{v}$
\end_inset

 is over all vertices in the lattice and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{p}$
\end_inset

 is over all plaquettes.
 Recall the Hamiltonian of a system specifies its total energy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Facts:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

 are all Hermitian
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 is Hermitian iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\langle H\psi\vert\psi\rangle=\langle\psi\vert H\psi\rangle=\langle H^{\dagger}\psi\vert\psi\rangle$
\end_inset

.
 Also sometimes called 
\emph on
self-adjoint.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and square to the identity
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}\cdot A_{v}^{\dagger}\equiv I$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Therefore they each (all) have eigenvalues of either 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Each of them commutes with all of the others
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 commutes with 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v'}$
\end_inset

 because they do not overlap at all
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

 commutes with 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p'}$
\end_inset

 overlap on at most one edge and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{j}$
\end_inset

 always commutes with 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{j'}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If some 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 overlaps with some 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

 then they overlap on two edges; the overlapping 
\begin_inset Formula $Z,X$
\end_inset

 (one pair on each edge) each anti-commute but since there are two such
 pairs, the effect is cancelled out
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Therefore, we can measure all the 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

 simultaneously
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
If operators 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 commute, then you can come up with a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

complete
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 set of basis states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{f_{n}g_{n}}$
\end_inset

 that are eigenstates of both 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

, and thus express any state of the quantum system as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=\sum_{n}\alpha_{n}\ket{f_{n}g_{n}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Measuring 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 you will get eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{m}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\alpha_{m}^{2}\right|$
\end_inset

 and the system will be in eigenstate 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{f_{m}g_{m}}$
\end_inset

 and similarly for 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, it doesn't matter which order you measure 
\begin_inset Formula $F,G$
\end_inset

 in, you will get a consistent result.
 This is the significance of commuting operators.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and the ground-state energy of 
\begin_inset Formula $H$
\end_inset

 (i.e., the lowest energy state) will be the product of the 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 eigenstates of all of the 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Since, if you plug 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}\equiv B_{p}\equiv1$
\end_inset

 into eqn.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:hamil"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 you get the largest negative sum.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All of this should be looking familiar given section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:The-stabilizer-formalism"

\end_inset

 and it should come as no surprise that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A_{v},B_{p}\right\} $
\end_inset

 (over all vertices and all faces) comprise a stabilizer subgroup (of the
 Pauli group 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{k}$
\end_inset

 on the lattice, where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is the number of particles captured by the lattice).
 So what's the dimension of the code space 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{S_{k}}$
\end_inset

 on this lattice with these stabilizers? Thinking a little bit imaginatively
 about 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A_{v},B_{p}\right\} $
\end_inset

 as a set of constraints gives some physical meaning to them: violating
 any of the constraints (i.e., a state generating a measurement of 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 from any of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A_{v},B_{p}\right\} $
\end_inset

) costs energy by virtue of the hamiltonian (any 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v},B_{p}$
\end_inset

 that measures 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 will add a positive term to the sum in eqn.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:hamil"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

).
 Thus, the ground-state of the system (i.e., the lowest energy state) should
 obey all of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

constraints
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 induced by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ A_{v},B_{p}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 All told the dimension of the code space is 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "introtoriccode"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Toric Codes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But what do the possible legal codes look like? Note (by the just preceding
 discussion on satisfying constraints), this is the same thing as asking
 what are the ground-states (lowest energy states) of the system.
 First, as a kind of warm up, consider only the 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 and the system state where each qubit is in the 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{0}$
\end_inset

 state.
 In such a state, all 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 would be measured in the 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 state as well.
 What happens if we apply 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 to one or two qubits in a row (i.e., flip two spins)? In this case only vertex
 operators 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 adjacent to those qubits will be affected, through the 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{j}$
\end_inset

 that measures 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 on the flipped qubit (see figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "spinflips"

\end_inset

 where in (a) we show how one such 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 is affected).
 Crucially, notice that for longer strings of errors (
\begin_inset Formula $\geq3$
\end_inset

 qubits) it's only the 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 at the ends of the string that will pick up the error.
 This ambiguity is what leads to techniques such as minimum weight perfect
 matching
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2105.13082"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 What happens if we flip a sequence of qubits that comprise a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

loop
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on the lattice? In that case, any 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 adjacent to any of the qubits will have 2 of its 4 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{j}$
\end_inset

 measure 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

 on the flipped qubits and but since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A_{v}:=Z_{j_{1}}Z_{j_{2}}Z_{j_{3}}Z_{j_{4}}\equiv\left(+1\times+1\right)\times\left(-1\times-1\right)\equiv+1
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

 will still be measured in its 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 eigenstate.
 
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename spinflips.png
	lyxscale 50
	width 70text%

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Flipped qubits
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 are shaded black.
 In (a) we consider a short string of flipped qubits and in (b) we consider
 a loop of flipped qubits.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "spinflips"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

Indeed there are many, many such possible states (with some number of non-flippe
d qubits but also loops of flipped qubits).
 This is called 
\emph on
degeneracy
\emph default
, when lots of distinct physical states are measured to have the same energy.
 In particular (you'll see why in a second), if we only measure 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

, loops that go over the edge and back aroud (i.e., all the way around the
 torus), will also be indistringuishable from the groundstate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that what we're trying to accomplish in constructing error correcting
 codes is to discover particular states of our quantum system (in this case,
 a lattice of spin-
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2}$
\end_inset

 particles) can be in that can be distinguished between using non-destructive
 measurements (in this case, using stabilizers).
 Therefore such degeneracy is problematic.
 The resolution is to apply the 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

, i.e., measure 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
as well as
\emph default
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{v}$
\end_inset

.
 What does this accomplish? Well looking at figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "spinflipswithbp"

\end_inset

, it seems like not much, but in fact we can use the 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

 to completely eliminate any loop of spin flips, by applying the 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{p}$
\end_inset

 adjacent to the interior edge of the loop (see figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "spinflipswithbp-1"

\end_inset

).
 
